__d(function(e,t,o,a,i,n,s){i.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2013 Class 7",chapter_number:13,chapter_title:"Exponents and Powers",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"In the expression 2\u2075, what is the base and what is the exponent?",back:"Base = 2 (the number being multiplied repeatedly). Exponent = 5 (how many times the base is multiplied by itself). 2\u2075 = 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 = 32.",memory_hook:"BASE is at the BOTTOM like the foundation of a building. EXPONENT is up HIGH like a flag. In 2\u2075 \u2014 2 is on the ground (base), 5 is flying up top (exponent)!"},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the value of any non-zero number raised to the power 0?",back:"Any non-zero number raised to the power 0 equals 1. a\u2070 = 1 (where a \u2260 0). Examples: 5\u2070 = 1, 100\u2070 = 1, (\u22127)\u2070 = 1, 2\u2070 = 1. Note: 0\u2070 is undefined.",memory_hook:"ZERO power = ONE result. Think: 'Zero-th power, One answer.' Or: a\u1d50 \xf7 a\u1d50 = a\u1d50\u207b\u1d50 = a\u2070 = 1 (since any number divided by itself = 1). That's WHY a\u2070 = 1!"},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"State the Product Rule of exponents with an example.",back:"Product Rule: When multiplying two powers with the SAME base, ADD the exponents. a\u1d50 \xd7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207a\u207f. Example: 3\u2074 \xd7 3\xb2 = 3\u2074\u207a\xb2 = 3\u2076 = 729. ONLY works when bases are identical. 2\xb3 \xd7 3\xb2 CANNOT use this rule.",memory_hook:"SAME base + MULTIPLY \u2192 ADD exponents. \xd7\u2192+ (multiplication becomes addition of powers). 'When you MULTIPLY powers, you ADD their heights!'"},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"State the Quotient Rule of exponents with an example.",back:"Quotient Rule: When dividing two powers with the SAME base, SUBTRACT the exponents. a\u1d50 \xf7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207b\u207f. Example: 5\u2077 \xf7 5\xb3 = 5\u2077\u207b\xb3 = 5\u2074 = 625. ONLY works when bases are identical and m \u2265 n.",memory_hook:"SAME base + DIVIDE \u2192 SUBTRACT exponents. \xf7\u2192\u2212 (division becomes subtraction of powers). 'When you DIVIDE powers, you SUBTRACT their heights!'"},{id:"fc05",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"State the Power of a Power rule with an example.",back:"Power of a Power: When a power is raised to another power, MULTIPLY the exponents. (a\u1d50)\u207f = a\u1d50\u02e3\u207f. Example: (2\xb3)\u2074 = 2\xb3\u02e3\u2074 = 2\xb9\xb2 = 4096. Think of it as applying the exponent m repeatedly n times.",memory_hook:"POWER on POWER \u2192 MULTIPLY exponents. ()\u2192\xd7 (bracket/power becomes multiplication). 'A power POWERED again gets MULTIPLIED!' (2\xb3)\u2074: the \xb3 and \u2074 get MARRIED (multiplied) \u2192 2\xb9\xb2."},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Evaluate: (\u22123)\xb3 and (\u22123)\u2074. What pattern do you notice?",back:"(\u22123)\xb3 = (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22123) = 9 \xd7 (\u22123) = \u221227. (Negative, odd power \u2192 result is NEGATIVE). (\u22123)\u2074 = (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22123) = 81. (Negative, even power \u2192 result is POSITIVE). Pattern: Even power of a negative = positive. Odd power of a negative = negative.",memory_hook:"Negative number raised to ODD power = NEGATIVE (odd one out). Negative raised to EVEN power = POSITIVE (pairs cancel negatives). Count the minuses: pairs of negatives cancel. ODD leftover = stays negative!"},{id:"fc07",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is 'standard form' (scientific notation)? Write 45,70,000 in standard form.",back:"Standard form expresses a number as a \xd7 10\u207f where 1 \u2264 a < 10 and n is a whole number. For 45,70,000: place decimal after first digit \u2192 4.570000; count places decimal moved left = 6; Standard form = 4.57 \xd7 10\u2076. Verify: 4.57 \xd7 10\u2076 = 45,70,000 \u2713.",memory_hook:"Standard form = 'one digit DOT rest \xd7 10^(count)'. Count how many places you MOVED the decimal point left \u2014 that count is the EXPONENT of 10!"},{id:"fc08",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why can't you apply the product rule to 2\xb3 \xd7 5\u2074?",back:"The product rule (a\u1d50 \xd7 a\u207f = a\u1d50\u207a\u207f) ONLY works when BOTH powers have the SAME base. Here 2\xb3 has base 2 and 5\u2074 has base 5 \u2014 different bases. You cannot combine them: 2\xb3 \xd7 5\u2074 = 8 \xd7 625 = 5000 (must evaluate separately). The rule literally says 'a\u1d50 \xd7 a\u207f' \u2014 the same 'a' appears in both.",memory_hook:"Product rule = FAMILY RULE. Only family members (same base = same family) can combine. 2\xb3 and 5\u2074 are from DIFFERENT families (2's family vs 5's family) \u2014 no combining allowed!"},{id:"fc09",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Write the prime factorisation of 360 in index (exponential) form.",back:"360 = 2 \xd7 180 = 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 90 = 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 45 = 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 9 \xd7 5 = 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 3 \xd7 3 \xd7 5. In index form: 360 = 2\xb3 \xd7 3\xb2 \xd7 5\xb9 = 2\xb3 \xd7 3\xb2 \xd7 5.",memory_hook:"360 degrees in a circle \u2014 and 360 = 2\xb3 \xd7 3\xb2 \xd7 5. Use a factor tree: 360 \u2192 2\xd7180 \u2192 2\xd72\xd790 \u2192 2\xd72\xd72\xd745 \u2192 2\xd72\xd72\xd79\xd75 \u2192 2\xd72\xd72\xd73\xd73\xd75. Count each prime: 2 appears 3 times, 3 appears 2 times, 5 appears once."},{id:"fc10",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How many zeros does 10\u2078 have? What number is it?",back:"10\u2078 = 10 \xd7 10 \xd7 10 \xd7 10 \xd7 10 \xd7 10 \xd7 10 \xd7 10 = 100,000,000 = ten crore. It has 8 zeros (one for each power). The rule: 10\u207f = 1 followed by n zeros. 10\xb9=10, 10\xb2=100, 10\xb3=1000, 10\u2074=10,000, 10\u2075=1,00,000, 10\u2076=10,00,000, 10\u2077=1,00,00,000, 10\u2078=10,00,00,000.",memory_hook:"10^n = 1 with n ZEROS after it. The exponent IS the zero-count. 10\u2078 \u2192 8 zeros \u2192 10,00,00,000. EASY: just write 1 and then count out n zeros!"},{id:"fc11",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Simplify: 2\u2075 \xd7 2\xb3 \xf7 2\u2074",back:"All bases are 2 \u2014 apply laws in order. Step 1: 2\u2075 \xd7 2\xb3 = 2\u2075\u207a\xb3 = 2\u2078 (product rule). Step 2: 2\u2078 \xf7 2\u2074 = 2\u2078\u207b\u2074 = 2\u2074 (quotient rule). Step 3: 2\u2074 = 16. Answer = 16.",memory_hook:"When all bases are same: collect EVERYTHING first using \xd7\u2192+ and \xf7\u2192\u2212. 2\u2075 \xd7 2\xb3 \xf7 2\u2074 = 2^(5+3\u22124) = 2\u2074 = 16. Add the multiplied ones, subtract the divided one!"},{id:"fc12",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Explain why 2\xb3 + 2\xb3 \u2260 2\u2076.",back:"2\xb3 + 2\xb3 = 8 + 8 = 16 = 2\u2074 (not 2\u2076). Laws of exponents apply to MULTIPLICATION and DIVISION, NOT to addition. 2\xb3 + 2\xb3 = 2 \xd7 2\xb3 = 2\xb9 \xd7 2\xb3 = 2\u2074 = 16. Compare: 2\xb3 \xd7 2\xb3 = 2\u2076 = 64. Addition and multiplication of powers give VERY different results. Never mix up + and \xd7 when working with exponents.",memory_hook:"ADDING powers \u2260 adding exponents. 2\xb3+2\xb3 = 16, NOT 2\u2076=64. Test: 8+8=16, but 2\u2076=64. Very different! Exponent laws are ONLY for \xd7 and \xf7 \u2014 NEVER for + and \u2212."},{id:"fc13",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is (ab)\u1d50 = ? Give an example.",back:"(ab)\u1d50 = a\u1d50 \xd7 b\u1d50. The exponent distributes over multiplication. Example: (2 \xd7 3)\u2074 = 2\u2074 \xd7 3\u2074 = 16 \xd7 81 = 1296. Verify: (2 \xd7 3)\u2074 = 6\u2074 = 1296 \u2713. Another: (3 \xd7 5)\xb2 = 3\xb2 \xd7 5\xb2 = 9 \xd7 25 = 225 = 15\xb2 \u2713.",memory_hook:"Power DISTRIBUTES over a product like sharing: (ab)\u1d50 gives each factor its own copy of the exponent. Like distributing pocket money: both a AND b get the full power m!"},{id:"fc14",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does India's population of about 140 crore look in standard form?",back:"140 crore = 1,40,00,00,000 (140 followed by 5 zeros after 140 crore = 1.4 billion). 1,40,00,00,000 = 1.4 \xd7 10\u2079. Place decimal after 1 \u2192 1.40000000; decimal moved 9 places left \u2192 10\u2079. India's population \u2248 1.4 \xd7 10\u2079. In words: 1.4 billion or 140 crore.",memory_hook:"India = 1.4 BILLION = 1.4 \xd7 10\u2079. Billion = 10\u2079 (9 zeros). Crore = 10\u2077 (7 zeros). Lakh = 10\u2075 (5 zeros). Know these! They come up in every newspaper and exam."},{id:"fc15",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the expanded form of 56,789 using powers of 10?",back:"56,789 = 5 \xd7 10,000 + 6 \xd7 1,000 + 7 \xd7 100 + 8 \xd7 10 + 9 \xd7 1 = 5 \xd7 10\u2074 + 6 \xd7 10\xb3 + 7 \xd7 10\xb2 + 8 \xd7 10\xb9 + 9 \xd7 10\u2070. Each digit is multiplied by the power of 10 corresponding to its place value. This is the link between exponents and our everyday place-value number system.",memory_hook:"PLACE VALUE = POWER of 10. Units = 10\u2070, Tens = 10\xb9, Hundreds = 10\xb2, Thousands = 10\xb3, Ten-thousands = 10\u2074. Your number system was built on exponents of 10 all along!"},{id:"fc16",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"The distance from Earth to the Moon is 3,84,000 km. Write this in standard form.",back:"3,84,000 = 3.84 \xd7 10\u2075 km. Place decimal after 3 \u2192 3.84000; decimal moved 5 places left \u2192 10\u2075. Standard form: 3.84 \xd7 10\u2075 km. Used by ISRO scientists in Bengaluru and every space agency worldwide \u2014 standard form makes space distances manageable.",memory_hook:"Moon distance = 3.84 \xd7 10\u2075 km. 10\u2075 = 1 lakh. So Moon is about 3.84 lakh km away. ISRO Bengaluru calculated this to land Chandrayaan-3 precisely!"},{id:"fc17",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A computer file is 2\xb9\u2070 KB. How many bytes is that? What is 2\xb9\u2070?",back:"1 KB = 2\xb9\u2070 bytes = 1024 bytes (not 1000 \u2014 computers use powers of 2!). So 2\xb9\u2070 KB = 2\xb9\u2070 \xd7 2\xb9\u2070 = (2\xb9\u2070)\xb2 = 2\xb2\u2070 bytes = 1,048,576 bytes \u2248 1 MB. This uses the Power of a Power rule: (2\xb9\u2070)\xb2 = 2\xb2\u2070. IT companies in Bengaluru (Infosys, Wipro) work with these numbers daily.",memory_hook:"2\xb9\u2070 = 1024 \u2248 1000 (the computer's 'kilo'). 2\xb2\u2070 \u2248 1,000,000 (mega). 2\xb3\u2070 \u2248 1,000,000,000 (giga). Computer storage is ALL powers of 2. Your 128 GB phone = 2\u2077 \xd7 2\xb3\u2070 = 2\xb3\u2077 bytes!"},{id:"fc18",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Evaluate: (3\xb2)\xb3 \xd7 3\u2070 \xf7 3\xb2",back:"Step 1: (3\xb2)\xb3 = 3\xb2\u02e3\xb3 = 3\u2076 (power of power). Step 2: 3\u2070 = 1 (zero exponent). Step 3: 3\u2076 \xd7 3\u2070 = 3\u2076 \xd7 1 = 3\u2076 (multiplying by 1). Step 4: 3\u2076 \xf7 3\xb2 = 3\u2076\u207b\xb2 = 3\u2074 (quotient rule). Step 5: 3\u2074 = 81. Answer = 81.",memory_hook:"Multi-law problems: do POWER OF POWER first, then ZERO EXPONENT (makes 1 \u2014 easy!), then PRODUCT and QUOTIENT rules. Order: (())\u2192 \u2070 \u2192 \xd7 \u2192 \xf7. Always simplify zero exponents early \u2014 they disappear into 1!"},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"If 2\u02e3 = 64, what is x?",back:"We need to find x such that 2\u02e3 = 64. Express 64 as a power of 2: 64 = 2 \xd7 32 = 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 16 = 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 8 = 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 4 = 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 \xd7 2 = 2\u2076. Therefore 2\u02e3 = 2\u2076, so x = 6.",memory_hook:"To find the unknown exponent: express BOTH sides as powers of the same base, then exponents MUST be equal. 2\u02e3 = 64 = 2\u2076 \u2192 x = 6. Like solving a= b equation \u2014 if bases match, powers match!"},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"The speed of light is 3,00,000 km/s. Write in standard form and find how far light travels in 1 hour.",back:"Speed = 3,00,000 km/s = 3 \xd7 10\u2075 km/s. 1 hour = 60 \xd7 60 = 3,600 seconds = 3.6 \xd7 10\xb3 s. Distance = speed \xd7 time = 3 \xd7 10\u2075 \xd7 3.6 \xd7 10\xb3 = (3 \xd7 3.6) \xd7 (10\u2075 \xd7 10\xb3) = 10.8 \xd7 10\u2078 = 1.08 \xd7 10\u2079 km = 1.08 billion km. Light travels about 1.08 \xd7 10\u2079 km in 1 hour!",memory_hook:"Light travels 3 \xd7 10\u2075 km every SECOND. In 1 hour (3.6 \xd7 10\xb3 seconds): multiply 3 \xd7 3.6 = 10.8 AND 10\u2075 \xd7 10\xb3 = 10\u2078. Together: 1.08 \xd7 10\u2079 km. Product rule at work in real physics!"}]}},2965,[]);